=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. A thorough end-to-end verification of every element the ticket points to confirms the network is fully operational.

**Connectivity:** Pings from host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) to every data-center target — web_server_0 through web_server_3 (10.200.0.3–.6) and dns_server (10.200.0.2) — all complete with 0% packet loss and normal latency (~2 ms). There is no loss of DC access.

**Uplink the user suspects:** The switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 link (172.16.0.6/31 on eth0/eth2 respectively) is physically UP on both sides, all interfaces show `state UP`, and OSPF has maintained a Full adjacency between switch_dist_1_1 (router-ID 172.16.0.7) and router_core_1 for over 2.5 minutes. No interface flap, no neighbour state below Full.

**OSPF convergence:** router_core_1 is in Full state with all three neighbours (router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1). All routing tables on every device (switch_dist_1_1, router_core_1, router_core_3, switch_server_access) hold complete routes to both office subnets and the DC subnet 10.200.0.0/24.

**Forwarding path confirmed:** Traceroute from host_1_1_1_1 traces the expected 5-hop path: host → switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 → router_core_3 → switch_server_access → web_server_0. No ACL or iptables rules exist on any device to block traffic.

**Root cause:** The helpdesk ticket is a false premise — the distribution uplink is intact and the data-center is fully reachable. No corrective action is required; the ticket should be closed and the user's workstation or local application should be investigated for the perceived connectivity issue.
=== END ===